7.21.m:
Hariśarman said:
Those gifts that bring happiness have been described by you, O Lord;
to whom are these gifts to be given? You ought to tell me that.

Brahmā said:
A brāhmaṇa is the supreme teacher of all castes;
to him gifts should be given by those endowed with devotion and faith.

The brāhmaṇa said:
The brāhmaṇa was declared by you to be the teacher of all castes, O best of gods;
yet among them, who is the most distinguished? To whom should the gift be given?

Brahmā said:
All brāhmaṇas are indeed distinguished and always worthy of worship;
[even] those brāhmaṇas who are afflicted with faults like theft, O best of brāhmaṇas,

and those who are hostile toward us, [should be given to] before others, never [refused].
Brāhmaṇas lacking good conduct are worthy of worship, but not śūdras who have conquered their senses;
even cows that eat inedible things are remembered as the mothers of the worlds.

go, brāhmaṇa—blessing be upon you—to the abode of the wielder of the discus.
Hariśarman said:
